+(duration-opts)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is either a whole
+number of seconds, such as `120`, or one or more groups of an integer followed by a unit: `d` for days, `h` for
+hours, `m` for minutes, and `s` for seconds. Each unit may be used at most once, units must appear in that order, and
+spaces are not allowed. Units are case-insensitive, so `1h30m` and `1H30M` are the same.
+
+| Value      | Result                                               |
+| ---------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
+| `120`      | `timedelta(seconds=120)`                             |
+| `45s`      | `timedelta(seconds=45)`                              |
+| `90m`      | `timedelta(minutes=90)`                              |
+| `1h30m`    | `timedelta(hours=1, minutes=30)`                     |
+| `2d`       | `timedelta(days=2)`                                  |
+| `1d2h3m4s` | `timedelta(days=1, hours=2, minutes=3, seconds=4)` |
+
+Negative numbers, decimals, unknown units, repeated or out of order units, and spaces are rejected. A
+{class}`~datetime.timedelta` passed as a default is used as is. The type shows as `DURATION` in help pages.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=["--help"])
+    invoke(wait, args=["--timeout=1m30s"])
+    invoke(wait, args=["--timeout=1h 30m"])
+```
+
